Each tick mark on the graph is one unit.

Determine the values of \( z \in \mathbb{C} \) for which \( A_2(z) = 0 \), where \( e^{jz} = A_0(z) + j A_1(z) + j^2 A_2(z) \) and:
\[
\begin{cases}
A_0(z) = \displaystyle \sum_{n \geq 0} \frac{x^{3n}}{(3n)!} \\
A_1(z) = \displaystyle \sum_{n \geq 0} \frac{x^{3n+1}}{(3n+1)!} \\
A_2(z) = \displaystyle \sum_{n \geq 0} \frac{x^{3n+2}}{(3n+2)!}
\end{cases}
\]
with \( j = e^{i \frac{2\pi}{3}} \).

Let $a,$ $b,$ $c,$ $d,$ and $e$ be the distinct roots of the equation $x^5 + 7x^4 - 2 = 0.$  Find
\begin{align*}
&\frac{a^3}{(a - b)(a - c)(a - d)(a - e)} + \frac{b^3}{(b - a)(b - c)(b - d)(b - e)} \\
&\quad + \frac{c^3}{(c - a)(c - b)(c - d)(c - e)} + \frac{d^3}{(d - a)(d - b)(d - c)(d - e)} \\
&\quad + \frac{e^3}{(e - a)(e - b)(e - c)(e - d)}.
\end{align*}

Triangle $ABC$ has a right angle at $B$, and contains a point $P$ for which $PA = 10$, $PB = 6$, and $\angle APB = \angle BPC = \angle CPA$. Find $PC$.

[asy]
unitsize(0.2 cm);

pair A, B, C, P;

A = (0,14);
B = (0,0);
C = (21*sqrt(3),0);
P = intersectionpoint(arc(B,6,0,180),arc(C,33,0,180));

draw(A--B--C--cycle);
draw(A--P);
draw(B--P);
draw(C--P);

label("$A$", A, NW);
label("$B$", B, SW);
label("$C$", C, SE);
label("$P$", P, NE);
[/asy]

Square $ABCD$ is constructed along diameter $AB$ of a semicircle, as shown. The semicircle and square $ABCD$ are coplanar. Line segment $AB$ has a length of 6 centimeters. If point $M$ is the midpoint of arc $AB$, what is the length of segment $MC$? Express your answer in simplest radical form. [asy]
size(4cm);

dotfactor = 4;
defaultpen(linewidth(1)+fontsize(10pt));

pair A,B,C,D,M;
A = (0,1);
B = (1,1);
C = (1,0);
D = (0,0);
M = (.5,1.5);

draw(A..M..B--C--D--cycle);
draw(A--B);

dot("A",A,W);
dot("M",M,N);
dot("B",B,E);
dot("C",C,E);
dot("D",D,W);

draw(M--C,linetype("0 4"));

[/asy]

Square A has a perimeter of $24$ cm.  Square B has an area equal to one-fourth the area of square A.  What is the perimeter of square B?

[asy]
draw((0,0)--(7,0));
draw((7,0)--(7,7));
draw((7,7)--(0,7));
draw((0,7)--(0,0));
draw((11,2)--(11,5));
draw((11,5)--(14,5));
draw((14,5)--(14,2));
draw((14,2)--(11,2));
label("A",(3.5,3.5));
label("B",(12.5,3.5));
[/asy]

Triangle $PAB$ and square $ABCD$ are in perpendicular planes. Given that $PA=3$, $PB=4$, and $AB=5$, what is $PD$? [asy]
size(100); defaultpen(linewidth(0.8)); dotfactor=4;
draw((0,-0.5)--(0,3)--(5,3)--(5,-0.5)--cycle);
filldraw((0,0)--(-2,-2)--(3,-2)--(5,0)--cycle,white,defaultpen);
draw((1.5,0)--(3.5,0)--(3.5,2)--(1.5,2)--cycle);
draw((1.5,0)--(1.6,-0.5)--(3.5,0));
dot("$B$",(1.5,0),SW); dot("$A$",(3.5,0),SE); dot("$C$",(1.5,2),W); dot("$D$",(3.5,2),E); dot("$P$",(1.6,-0.5),S);
[/asy]

Consider the $2\pi$-periodic function $f:\Bbb{R}\to\Bbb{R}$ defined as follows:

$$f(x) = \begin{cases} 
1 & \text{if } x = 0 \\
1 + \sin\left(\frac{\pi^2}{x}\right) & \text{if } x \in [-\pi,\pi]\setminus\{0\}
\end{cases}$$

Determine whether the Fourier series of $f$ converges at $x=0$. If it converges, find the sum at $x=0$. \( \boxed{} \)

Determine the number of triples of natural numbers \((a, b, c)\) that satisfy the following system of equations:

\[\begin{cases}
\gcd(a, b, c) = 15 \\
\text{lcm}(a, b, c) = 3^{15} \cdot 5^{18}
\end{cases}\]

The field shown has been planted uniformly with wheat. [asy]
draw((0,0)--(1/2,sqrt(3)/2)--(3/2,sqrt(3)/2)--(2,0)--(0,0),linewidth(0.8));
label("$60^\circ$",(0.06,0.1),E);
label("$120^\circ$",(1/2-0.05,sqrt(3)/2-0.1),E);
label("$120^\circ$",(3/2+0.05,sqrt(3)/2-0.1),W);
label("$60^\circ$",(2-0.05,0.1),W);
label("100 m",(1,sqrt(3)/2),N);
label("100 m",(1.75,sqrt(3)/4+0.1),E);
[/asy] At harvest, the wheat at any point in the field is brought to the nearest point on the field's perimeter. What is the fraction of the crop that is brought to the longest side?

A rectangular piece of paper $ABCD$ is folded so that edge $CD$ lies along edge $AD,$ making a crease $DP.$ It is unfolded, and then folded again so that edge $AB$ lies along edge $AD,$ making a second crease $AQ.$ The two creases meet at $R,$ forming triangles $PQR$ and $ADR$. If $AB=5\mbox{ cm}$ and $AD=8\mbox{ cm},$ what is the area of quadrilateral $DRQC,$ in $\mbox{cm}^2?$

[asy]
size(250);
draw((0,0)--(5,0)--(5,8)--(0,8)--cycle,black+linewidth(1));
draw((8,0)--(8,8)--(13,8)--(13,5)--(8,5),black+linewidth(1));
draw((8,0)--(13,5),black+linewidth(1)+dashed);
draw((16,0)--(21,0)--(21,8)--(16,8)--cycle,black+linewidth(1));
draw((16,0)--(21,5),black+linewidth(1)+dashed);
draw((16,8)--(21,3),black+linewidth(1)+dashed);
draw((12,0)--(10,2),black+linewidth(1));
draw((12,0)--(10,2),EndArrow);
label("$A$",(0,8),N);
label("$B$",(5,8),N);
label("$C$",(5,0),S);
label("$D$",(0,0),S);
label("$A$",(8,8),N);
label("$B$",(13,8),N);
label("$C$",(8,5),W);
label("$D$",(8,0),S);
label("$P$",(13,5),E);
label("$A$",(16,8),N);
label("$B$",(21,8),N);
label("$C$",(21,0),S);
label("$D$",(16,0),S);
label("$P$",(21,5),E);
label("$Q$",(21,3),E);
label("$R$",(20,4),W);
[/asy]

Below are the graphs of two functions, $f(x)$ and $g(x)$, defined on the domain $0\le x\le 18$:

[asy]
import graph; size(8cm); real lsf=0.5; pen dps=linewidth(0.7)+fontsize(10); defaultpen(dps); pen ds=black; real xmin=-1.5,xmax=18.5,ymin=-1.5,ymax=12.5;

pen cqcqcq=rgb(0.75,0.75,0.75);

/*grid*/ pen gs=linewidth(0.7)+cqcqcq+linetype("2 2"); real gx=1,gy=1;
for(real i=ceil(xmin/gx)*gx;i<=floor(xmax/gx)*gx;i+=gx) draw((i,ymin)--(i,ymax),gs); for(real i=ceil(ymin/gy)*gy;i<=floor(ymax/gy)*gy;i+=gy) draw((xmin,i)--(xmax,i),gs);

Label laxis; laxis.p=fontsize(10);

xaxis("",xmin,xmax,Ticks(laxis,Step=2.0,Size=2,NoZero),Arrows(6),above=true); yaxis("",ymin,ymax,Ticks(laxis,Step=2.0,Size=2,NoZero),Arrows(6),above=true);
draw((0,10)--(2,10)--(8,4)--(10,4)--(18,12),darkgreen+1.5);
draw((0,2)--(6,8)--(10,8)--(12,10)--(18,10),orange+dashed+1.5);
[/asy]

If the graph of $f(x)$ is the dashed orange line, and the graph of $g(x)$ is the solid green line, what is the largest value of $f(x)-g(x)$?

In triangle $ABC,$ $M$ is the midpoint of $\overline{BC},$ $AB = 12,$ and $AC = 16.$  Let $E$ be on $\overline{AC},$ and $F$ be on $\overline{AB},$ and let $G$ be the intersection of $\overline{EF}$ and $\overline{AM}.$  If $AE = 2AF,$ then find $\frac{EG}{GF}.$

[asy]
unitsize(0.3 cm);

pair A, B, C, E, F, G, M;
real x = 4;

B = (0,0);
C = (18,0);
A = intersectionpoint(arc(B,12,0,180),arc(C,16,0,180));
M = (B + C)/2;
F = interp(A,B,x/12);
E = interp(A,C,2*x/16);
G = extension(E,F,A,M);

draw(A--B--C--cycle);
draw(E--F);
draw(A--M);

label("$A$", A, N);
label("$B$", B, SW);
label("$C$", C, SE);
label("$E$", E, NE);
label("$F$", F, NW);
label("$G$", G, SW);
label("$M$", M, S);
[/asy]

Two circles have the same center O. Point X is the midpoint of segment OP. What is the ratio of the area of the circle with radius OX to the area of the circle with radius OP? Express your answer as a common fraction.

[asy]
import graph;
draw(Circle((0,0),20));
draw(Circle((0,0),12));
dot((0,0));
dot((20,0));
dot((12,0));
draw((0,0)--(20,0));
label("$O$",(0,0),SE);
label("$P$",(20,0),E);
label("$X$",(12,0),SE);
[/asy]

A square with sides 6 inches is shown. If $P$ is a point such that the segment $\overline{PA}$, $\overline{PB}$, $\overline{PC}$ are equal in length, and segment $\overline{PC}$ is perpendicular to segment $\overline{FD}$, what is the area, in square inches, of triangle $APB$? [asy]
pair A, B, C, D, F, P;
A = (0,0); B= (2,0); C = (1,2); D = (2,2); F = (0,2); P = (1,1);
draw(A--B--D--F--cycle);
draw(C--P); draw(P--A); draw(P--B);
label("$A$",A,SW); label("$B$",B,SE);label("$C$",C,N);label("$D$",D,NE);label("$P$",P,NW);label("$F$",F,NW);
label("$6''$",(1,0),S);

[/asy]

The graph of $x^2 - 2xy + 3y^2 = 5$ is an ellipse, although its axes are not parallel to the coordinate axes. Two horizontal lines and two vertical lines lie tangent to the ellipse, forming a rectangle, as shown:
[asy]
size(7cm);
draw(rotate(20)*xscale(2.4)*unitcircle);
draw((-3.5,0)--(3.5,0),EndArrow);
draw((0,-2.5)--(0,2.5),EndArrow);
real r1=2.29; draw((r1,-2.2)--(r1,2.2),dotted);
draw((-r1,-2.2)--(-r1,2.2),dotted);
real r2=1.26; draw((-3,r2)--(3,r2),dotted);
draw((-3,-r2)--(3,-r2),dotted);
label("$x$",(3.5,0),E); label("$y$",(0,2.5),N);
[/asy]
What is the area of the rectangle?

In the graph below, each grid line counts as one unit. The line shown below passes through the point $(1001,n)$ (not shown on graph).  Find $n$.
[asy]size(250,0);
add(shift(-10,-10)*grid(20,20));
draw((-10,0)--(10,0),linewidth(2));
draw((0,-10)--(0,10),linewidth(2));
label("x",(10,0),E);
label("y",(0,10),N);
draw((-10,-2.71) -- (10,8.71),blue,Arrows);[/asy]
